NOTICE — third-party attribution for the NMS ESP32 firmware probe
=================================================================

This firmware discharges the attribution obligation in Probe Protocol v1 §2.1,
which names ESP32-Bit-Pirate as a consulted design reference. Its source was read
for read-only radio and network-recon patterns; the mapping below records exactly
what was borrowed and what was deliberately not, so the citation is specific
rather than a bare link (firmware spec §6.4, §9).


ESP32-Bit-Pirate
----------------
  Project:  ESP32 Bit Pirate
  Author:   geo-tp
  Source:   https://github.com/geo-tp/ESP32-Bit-Pirate
  License:  MIT (full text reproduced below)

Techniques adopted (recon/radio primitives — established prior art, not claimed
by this project; firmware spec §6.4):

  Bit-Pirate command   Technique borrowed                          Our command
  ------------------   -----------------------------------------   ------------------------
  scan                 Active AP scan (BSSID, channel, RSSI,       wifi_survey (AP list)
                       auth, hidden/open flags)
  sniff                Passive promiscuous capture, channel-hop    wifi_survey (client list)
  sniff (extended)     Promiscuous frame classification by         wifi_ids (passive IDS:
                       type/subtype for security events            deauth-flood/rogue-AP/
                                                                   evil-twin detection)
  scan (BLE)           BLE device enumeration + AD payload parse   ble_scan
  discovery            ICMP sweep of a local subnet                discover
  ping                 ICMP echo for reachability/latency          monitor `ping` check
  nmap                 TCP connect scan (open/closed/filtered)     port_scan, monitor `port`
  nc                   Raw TCP connect to read a service banner    banner_grab
  lookup mac           OUI -> vendor resolution                    server-side enrichment
                                                                   (spec §6.5; NOT on-probe)

Reference points read while implementing the radio runners (paths are in the
upstream tree): BluetoothService.cpp (BLE enumeration) and WifiService.cpp
(promiscuous capture / frame handling).

The near one-to-one correspondence with the recon primitives is intentional: what
this project contributes is everything around them — the fleet, the protocol, the
persistence, and the multi-vantage correlation — which a single handheld tool
structurally cannot do.

Deliberately NOT adopted:

  * The raw 802.11 deauthentication attack (Bit-Pirate `deauth` /
    WifiService::deauthAttack, esp_wifi_80211_tx frame injection). Transmitting
    deauth frames is a wireless denial-of-service affecting every client in RF
    range, not an isolated bench. The `wifi_deauth` runner is NOT registered in
    the worker dispatch table, is never advertised in `announce.capabilities`,
    and cannot be dispatched by this firmware. See the standing decision recorded
    in firmware/src/worker.cpp.
  * The interactive-console commands (ssh, telnet, modbus, http analyze, webui,
    waterfall). They serve a one-operator handheld tool; a headless fleet node
    reporting to a C2 server has no on-device shell.

